  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Desk Companion",
    "overview": "The kiosk is a friendly character who keeps the owner company on the desk. The sunflower face is the presence; the cream cards beside it are the things it is holding up to show you. The world borrows from picture books (flat cobalt ground, cream pages, a thick dark-ink outline, a soft wonky serif for anything said aloud), but the job is companionship at a glance, not storytelling: the face reads from two metres away, and so does whatever it shows.\n\nDensity is low on purpose. Large type, few marks, generous gaps, and one idea per card. Colour is flat and saturated: cobalt carries the page, cream carries content, sunflower marks what is live or highlighted, and tomato is kept for what demands attention. Everything is drawn with the same ink line, so a new card looks like it came from the same hand.\n\nThe look the world rules out is dashboard grey: dim slate text, small uppercase eyebrow labels and hairline borders on dark panels. That is the `plain` theme's job, and it must not creep back into this one.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Flat cobalt ground, cream cards, one dark-ink outline weight (3px).",
      "A sunflower face that changes tint and motion with the agent's state.",
      "Fraunces (soft, wonky axes on) for the spoken line, titles and figures; Atkinson Hyperlegible Next for everything read.",
      "Depth only from ink: outlines on everything, solid ink drops only under things you can press.",
      "Big, few, legible from across a desk."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two Grounds Rule",
        "section": "colors",
        "body": "Text sits on cobalt or on cream, never anywhere else. On cobalt it is Cream or Periwinkle Dim; on cream it is Ink or Slate Ink Dim."
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sunflower Means Live Rule",
        "section": "colors",
        "body": "Sunflower marks what is live or highlighted. One sunflower highlight per card at most; a card that is mostly yellow has said nothing."
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tomato Is an Alarm Rule",
        "section": "colors",
        "body": "Tomato fills a surface only for a claim card. Elsewhere it is a dot, a delta or a series, never decoration."
      },
      {
        "name": "The Spoken Serif Rule",
        "section": "typography",
        "body": "Fraunces is for what the companion says or names: the spoken line, titles, figures, the start button. Anything read as data or instructions is Atkinson."
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Case Rule",
        "section": "typography",
        "body": "Titles are sentence case at full title size. No small uppercase tracked labels (`--title-case: none`)."
      },
      {
        "name": "The Press-Only Drop Rule",
        "section": "elevation",
        "body": "Only tappable things get an ink drop. Cards, panels and the face stay flat with outlines; a dropped card would read as a button."
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do set colour, type and surfaces through the tokens in `core/layout.css`; a theme overrides tokens first and adds signature rules only after.",
      "Do outline every new mark in Ink at the existing weights (3px surfaces, 2.5px small marks).",
      "Do use Fraunces with `SOFT 100, WONK 1` for spoken lines, titles and figures; Atkinson for everything read.",
      "Do keep text on the two grounds: Cream or Periwinkle Dim on cobalt, Ink or Slate Ink Dim on cream.",
      "Do check a new card at a desk's distance in all three presets and in portrait."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't let dashboard grey back in: no dim slate text on dark panels, no small uppercase tracked labels, no hairline borders.",
      "Don't give cards, panels or the face an ink drop; drops are for things you press.",
      "Don't fill a surface with Tomato except a claim card.",
      "Don't put more than one Sunflower highlight in a card.",
      "Don't style inside the face from a theme; dress it through the `--face-*` variables."
    ]
  }
}